# `docs/superhuman/` is a MOUNT POINT, not a directory this repo owns.
#
# Per-project superhuman working docs (VISION / REQUIREMENTS / DESIGN / PLAN /
# TEST / SUPERHUMAN / DECISIONS, and any other per-project notes) record a
# specific operator's environments, hosts, and internal vocabulary as a matter
# of course. This repo is published, so they must never become tracked files
# here. They are versioned — just by a different owner: the private repo
# OrionAIDev/superhuman-project-docs is cloned into this path, and nothing
# about that clone is visible to this repo. Deliberately a plain nested clone
# and not a submodule: a submodule would write the private URL into a tracked
# .gitmodules and hand every public cloner a fetch that 403s. See
# OrionAIDev/trapezia-roadmap#225.
#
# The publication guard in tests/test_content.py scans `git ls-files`, which
# means untracked content is invisible to it by construction — this rule is
# what keeps it that way, and
# tests/test_content.py::test_no_per_project_docs_are_tracked fails the build
# if anything under here ever becomes tracked (including a gitlink, if someone
# force-adds the nested clone).
#
# The whole directory, not `*/`: the previous rule matched subdirectories only,
# so a file dropped directly at docs/superhuman/FOO.md was never ignored.
#
# Anchored with a leading slash so the synthetic-bug fixture under
# tests/smoke/.../docs/superhuman/ is unaffected.
/docs/superhuman/

# Golden POLICY fixtures pin one operator's real environment names and their
# expected verdicts. ladder-generic.yaml pins mechanism and IS published; these
# are the installation-specific counterpart and must stay local — the original
# design called them "on the pre-publication removal list" (see that file's
# header), and this rule is what enforces it mechanically. The test that reads
# them skips when absent.
/tests/fixtures/golden/
